\chapter{The Basis inventory and compatibility layers}
\label{ch:51}
\index{The Basis inventory and compatibility layers}

The standard library uses the SML Basis inventory as a catalogue and implements On1x-specific subsets. A module name alone does not establish complete Standard ML semantics. Check the compatibility and coverage tables before relying on an operation or signature. Directory initializers expose package members, and small compatibility entry points such as \OnxCodeTerm{std.Int} coexist with the grouped source tree.

\section{Select an interface by behavior}
For a list pipeline, establish what the elements represent. The legacy Basis list operations use proper S-expression lists, so they have different shape and error behavior from a native heterogeneous tuple. The legacy \OnxCodeTerm{std.Option} represents a tagged tuple with a default payload. The pervasive Option is a nominal type with its own helper rules. A successful migration should preserve the meaning of absence and avoid running callbacks on absent inputs. Check signatures and a fixture before porting code by replacing module names. Refresh-generated hashes describe the catalogue's input files, while per-operation tests describe executed behavior.

\section{Port a lookup without reserving a sentinel}
Suppose a lookup may legitimately return an empty list. With the legacy Option facade, construct two test inputs: \OnxCodeTerm{some(sx\_nil())} and \OnxCodeTerm{none(sx\_nil())}, using the imported module's qualifiers. Projecting the second component of both tuples gives the same empty list. Projecting the first gives true in one case and false in the other. Thus an implementation that calls a result absent whenever the payload is empty loses information, even though it might pass every test whose successful lookup returned a nonempty list.

For a more discriminating test, map a function that announces each invocation and returns the length of the list. Supply zero as the output default. The present empty list should invoke the function and yield a present zero; the absent input should skip it and yield an absent tuple with zero in its payload slot. Comparing only the two numbers again erases the distinction. Compare the tags and the invocation counts. A porter replacing the tuple API by the pervasive nominal API should carry those observations across; recreating the unused default payload in the new representation is not the goal.

This exercise also gives a way to read the inventory. The compatibility entry describes the representation; the public Option facade tells you which operations are exported; the implementation of map shows exactly which branch calls the function. None of these is interchangeable with a catalogue entry saying that an SML signature is specified.

As a quick review question, what information is lost by calling the legacy fallback helper first with zero as its default? Both outcomes become the same ordinary integer before the caller can inspect their tags.

